{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"O07732","secondaryAccessions":["I6Y7T0"],"uniProtkbId":"LIPJ_MYCTU","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"2023-06-28","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-11-04","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"1997-07-01","entryVersion":158,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":5.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis (strain ATCC 25618 / H37Rv)","taxonId":83332,"lineage":["Bacteria","Bacillati","Actinomycetota","Actinomycetes","Mycobacteriales","Mycobacteriaceae","Mycobacterium","Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Bifunctional lipase/adenylate cyclase LipJ"}},"includes":[{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}],"value":"Lipase"},"ecNumbers":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}],"value":"3.1.1.-"}]}},{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"}],"value":"Adenylate cyclase"},"ecNumbers":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"}],"value":"4.6.1.1"}]},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Adenylyl cyclase"}}]}]},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000312","source":"EMBL","id":"CCP44667.1"}],"value":"lipJ"},"orderedLocusNames":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000312","source":"EMBL","id":"CCP44667.1"}],"value":"Rv1900c"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"34019937"}],"value":"May play a role in cell wall modulation (PubMed:33960821, PubMed:34019937). The N-terminal domain exhibits lipolytic activity (PubMed:33960821). In vitro, hydrolyzes various p-nitrophenyl (pNP) esters (PubMed:33960821). pNP-decanoate (C10) is the best substrate, followed by pNP-octonate (C8), pNP-laurate (C12) and pNP-butyrate (C4) (PubMed:33960821). Exhibits lower activity with pNP-acetate (C2), pNP-myristate (C14) and pNP-palmitate (C16) (PubMed:33960821). Can also use tributyrin (PubMed:33960821). The C-terminal domain catalyzes the biosynthesis of cyclic AMP (cAMP) from ATP (PubMed:15678099). It has a guanylyl cyclase side-activity, corresponding to 7% of the adenylate cyclase activity (PubMed:15678099)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"a fatty acid ester + H2O = an aliphatic alcohol + a fatty acid + H(+)","reactionCrossReferences":[{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:59388"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:2571"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15377"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15378"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:28868"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:35748"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}]}},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"ATP = 3',5'-cyclic AMP + diphosphate","reactionCrossReferences":[{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:15389"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:30616"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:33019"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:58165"}],"ecNumber":"4.6.1.1","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"}]},"physiologicalReactions":[{"directionType":"left-to-right","reactionCrossReference":{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:15390"},"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"}]}]},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"decanoate ester + H2O = an aliphatic alcohol + decanoate + H(+)","reactionCrossReferences":[{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:47360"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:2571"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15377"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15378"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:27689"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:87658"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}]}},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"an octanoate ester + H2O = an aliphatic alcohol + octanoate + H(+)","reactionCrossReferences":[{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:47356"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:2571"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15377"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15378"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:25646"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:87657"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}]}},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"a dodecanoate ester + H2O = an aliphatic alcohol + dodecanoate + H(+)","reactionCrossReferences":[{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:47364"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:2571"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15377"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15378"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:18262"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:87659"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}]}},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"a butanoate ester + H2O = an aliphatic alcohol + butanoate + H(+)","reactionCrossReferences":[{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:47348"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:2571"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15377"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15378"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:17968"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:50477"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}]}},{"commentType":"COFACTOR","cofactors":[{"name":"Mn(2+)","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"}],"cofactorCrossReference":{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:29035"}}],"note":{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"}],"value":"Cannot use Mg(2+) as a cofactor."}]}},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}],"value":"The lipolytic activity of the full-length protein is regulated/inhibited by the presence of the C-terminal adenylate cyclase domain (PubMed:33960821). Lipolytic activity of the full-length protein decreases in the presence of ATP, but ATP does not affect activity of the N-terminal domain in the absence of the C-terminal cyclase domain (PubMed:33960821). Lipolytic activity is inhibited by the serine modifier phenylmethylsulfonyl fluoride (PMSF) (PubMed:33960821)"}],"commentType":"ACTIVITY REGULATION"},{"commentType":"BIOPHYSICOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES","kineticParameters":{"maximumVelocities":[{"velocity":500.0,"unit":"nmol/min/mg","enzyme":"enzyme (for adenylate cyclase activity)","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"}]},{"velocity":1300.0,"unit":"nmol/min/mg","enzyme":"enzyme (for C-terminal cyclase domain and adenylate cyclase activity)","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"}]}],"michaelisConstants":[{"constant":69.0,"unit":"uM","substrate":"pNP-decanoate","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}]},{"constant":83.0,"unit":"uM","substrate":"pNP-decanoate (for N-terminal lipolytic domain)","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}]}],"note":{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}],"value":"kcat is 140 min(-1) for lipolytic activity. kcat is 355 min(-1) for N-terminal lipolytic domain and lipolytic activity."}]}},"phDependence":{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}],"value":"Optimum pH is 9.0 for lipolytic activity of the full-length protein."}]},"temperatureDependence":{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"}],"value":"Optimum temperature is 40 degrees Celsius for lipolytic activity with pNP-decanoate as substrate (PubMed:33960821). Optimum temperature is 45 degrees Celsius for adenylate cyclase activity (PubMed:15678099)."}]}},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"}],"value":"The C-terminal adenylyl cyclase domain forms homodimers (PubMed:15678099). Homodimers are asymmetric in both unliganded and ligand-bound states (PubMed:15678099)"}],"commentType":"SUBUNIT"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"34019937"}],"value":"Expression is up-regulated in acidic, nutritive and iron stress in M.tuberculosis H37Ra"}],"commentType":"INDUCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15678099"}],"value":"Composed of an N-terminal alpha/beta-hydrolase domain and a C-terminal cyclase homology domain (CHD)"}],"commentType":"DOMAIN"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33960821"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"34019937"}],"value":"Expression in M.smegmatis leads to altered colony morphology, growth, cell surface properties and cell wall permeability (PubMed:33960821, PubMed:34019937). M.smegmatis shows enhanced biofilm formation and survival under lysozyme, acidic, nutritive and antibiotic stress conditions (PubMed:33960821, PubMed:34019937). It also enhances the infection capability and the intracellular survival of mycobacteria (PubMed:34019937)"}],"commentType":"MISCELLANEOUS"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"In the N-terminal section; belongs to the AB hydrolase superfamily"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"PROSITE-ProRule","id":"PRU00099"}],"value":"In the C-terminal section; belongs to the adenylyl cyclase class-4/guanylyl cyclase family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":462,"modifier":"EXACT"}},